.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt MAN 7
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm man
.Nd legacy formatting language for manual pages
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
language describes a manual page by how it looks: headings, paragraphs,
indentation and fonts.
It is older than
.Xr mdoc 7 ,
in which OxideBSD's own pages are written, but most manual pages of
third-party software use it, and
.Xr oxdoc 1
formats both.
Like
.Xr mdoc 7 ,
it is written as
.Xr roff 7
input: text lines, and macro lines starting with a dot and a macro name.
.Pp
A macro's arguments are separated by spaces; an argument containing spaces
is quoted with double quotes, and a double quote inside one is written
twice.
Spaces inside a quoted argument are kept as they are, and the line may
break at any of them.
.Pp
Text lines are filled and adjusted into paragraphs.
A sentence should end at the end of an input line, so that it can be
followed by two spaces.
A blank line is a paragraph break with vertical space, like
.Ic \&sp ,
but
.Ic \&PP
should be used instead.
.Sh MANUAL STRUCTURE
A page starts with
.Ic \&TH
and continues with sections, conventionally NAME, SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION
and those listed in
.Xr mdoc 7 .
The NAME section holds one line: the names the page describes, separated by
commas, then
.Ql \e-
and a short description.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.TH NAME 1 2026-09-27 "OxideBSD" "General Commands Manual"
\&.SH NAME
name \e- one-line description
\&.SH SYNOPSIS
\&.B name
\&.RI [ file ...]
\&.SH DESCRIPTION
The
\&.B name
utility ...
.Ed
.Sh MACRO OVERVIEW
.Ss Document structure
.Bl -column "UR, UE" description -compact
.It Ic \&TH Ta the title line
.It Ic \&SH Ta section heading
.It Ic \&SS Ta subsection heading
.El
.Ss Paragraphs and indentation
.Bl -column "UR, UE" description -compact
.It Ic \&PP , \&LP , \&P Ta paragraph
.It Ic \&TP Ta paragraph with a tag on the next line
.It Ic \&TQ Ta another tag for the same paragraph
.It Ic \&IP Ta paragraph, optionally with a tag
.It Ic \&HP Ta paragraph with a hanging indent
.It Ic \&RS , \&RE Ta move the left margin right and back
.It Ic \&PD Ta the space between paragraphs
.El
.Ss Fonts
.Bl -column "UR, UE" description -compact
.It Ic \&B Ta bold
.It Ic \&I Ta italic
.It Ic \&SB Ta small bold, shown bold
.It Ic \&SM Ta small, shown in the regular font
.It Ic \&R Ta the regular font
.It Ic \&BI , \&BR , \&IB , \&IR , \&RB , \&RI Ta two fonts in turn
.El
.Ss Hyperlinks and cross-references
.Bl -column "UR, UE" description -compact
.It Ic \&UR , \&UE Ta a link to a URL
.It Ic \&MT , \&ME Ta a link to an e-mail address
.It Ic \&MR Ta a reference to another manual page
.El
.Ss Synopses and examples
.Bl -column "UR, UE" description -compact
.It Ic \&SY , \&YS Ta a command synopsis
.It Ic \&OP Ta an optional command-line option
.It Ic \&EX , \&EE Ta an example, not filled
.El
.Ss Obsolete macros
.Bl -column "UR, UE" description -compact
.It Ic \&AT Ta the AT&T system the page belongs to
.It Ic \&UC Ta the Berkeley system the page belongs to
.It Ic \&DT Ta restore the default tab stops
.El
.Sh MACRO REFERENCE
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic \&TH Ar title section Op Ar date Op Ar source Op Ar volume
The title line, which must come first.
.Ar title
is conventionally in capitals.
The page header shows it with
.Ar section
in parentheses at both ends and
.Ar volume
in the middle; the footer shows
.Ar source ,
the system or package the page belongs to, at the left,
.Ar date
in the middle and the title again at the right.
Without
.Ar volume ,
the section's usual name is used, such as
.Dq General Commands Manual
for section 1.
.It Ic \&SH Op Ar heading
A section heading, printed at the left margin in bold.
Without
.Ar heading ,
the next input line is the heading.
It ends any open paragraph and
.Ic \&RS
indentation.
.It Ic \&SS Op Ar heading
A subsection heading, indented less than the text of the section; a long
one wraps to the text's indentation.
.It Ic \&PP , \&LP , \&P
A paragraph: a blank line, then text at the section's indentation.
Empty paragraphs, and one right after a heading, produce no blank line.
.It Ic \&TP Op Ar width
A tagged paragraph.
The next input line is the tag, printed at the current indentation; the
text after it is indented by
.Ar width ,
by default seven columns or the width last given.
When the tag is too wide for that indentation, the text starts on the next
line.
Blank lines between
.Ic \&TP
and the tag are ignored.
.It Ic \&TQ
Another tag for the paragraph of the
.Ic \&TP
before it, printed on its own line with no space above it.
.It Ic \&IP Op Ar tag Op Ar width
An indented paragraph, with the optional
.Ar tag
in the margin like
.Ic \&TP .
.It Ic \&HP Op Ar width
A paragraph whose first line starts at the current indentation and whose
other lines are indented by
.Ar width .
.It Ic \&RS Op Ar width
Moves the left margin right by
.Ar width ,
by default the width of the last tagged paragraph, until the matching
.Ic \&RE .
.It Ic \&RE Op Ar level
Ends the innermost
.Ic \&RS ,
or all of them down to
.Ar level .
.It Ic \&PD Op Ar distance
Sets the vertical space before paragraphs and headings, in lines; without
.Ar distance ,
one line.
.Ql \&.PD 0
is used before a
.Ic \&TP
that should follow the one before it without space.
.It Ic \&B , \&I , \&SB , \&SM , \&R Op Ar text ...
Prints
.Ar text ,
or without it the next input line, in the macro's font.
.It Ic \&BI , \&BR , \&IB , \&IR , \&RB , \&RI Ar text ...
Prints its arguments joined without spaces, in the two fonts of its name in
turn: bold, italic or regular.
For example,
.Ql \&.BR ls (1)
prints
.Sy ls
in bold and
.Ql (1)
in the regular font.
.It Ic \&UR Ar url
The text up to
.Ic \&UE
is a link to
.Ar url ,
which is printed after it in angle brackets.
.It Ic \&UE Op Ar punctuation
Ends a
.Ic \&UR
link;
.Ar punctuation
is printed right after it.
.It Ic \&MT Ar address , Ic \&ME Op Ar punctuation
The same for an e-mail address.
.It Ic \&MR Ar name section Op Ar suffix
A reference to the manual page
.Ar name
in
.Ar section ,
as
.Ql name(section) .
.It Ic \&SY Ar command
Starts a synopsis of
.Ar command :
the command in bold, then the following text with its continuation lines
indented to line up after it.
.It Ic \&YS
Ends a synopsis.
.It Ic \&OP Ar flag Op Ar argument
An optional option in a synopsis: the flag in bold and the argument in
italics, in brackets.
.It Ic \&EX , \&EE
An example: the lines between them are printed as they are, not filled.
.It Ic \&AT Op Ar version Op Ar release
Sets the footer's source to an AT&T system:
.Dq 7th Edition
by default, or System III or System V for version 4 or 5.
.It Ic \&UC Op Ar version
Sets the footer's source to a Berkeley system, from
.Dq 3rd Berkeley Distribution
for version 3 to
.Dq 4.4 Berkeley Distribution
for version 7.
.It Ic \&DT
Restores the default tab stops, every half inch (five columns).
.El
.Pp
A font macro or a line of text ending in
.Ql \ec
continues on the next line without a space.
.Sh ROFF REQUESTS
These
.Xr roff 7
requests are commonly used in manual pages and have their usual effect on a
terminal:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic \&br
Ends the output line.
.It Ic \&sp Op Ar distance
Ends the line and leaves
.Ar distance
blank lines, by default one; an exact half line leaves none.
.It Ic \&nf , \&fi
Stops and resumes filling: in between, each input line is an output line.
.It Ic \&in Op Oo \(pl|\- Oc Ns Ar width
Sets the left margin: to
.Ar width
columns from the left edge of the page, or moved by it with a sign, or with
no argument back to the paragraph's own margin.
The next paragraph or heading macro resets it.
.It Ic \&ti Op Oo \(pl|\- Oc Ns Ar width
Indents only the next output line.
.It Ic \&ft Op Ar font
Changes the font, like
.Ic \ef ;
an unknown font name is ignored.
.It Ic \&ta Ar stop ...
Sets tab stops, in columns from the left margin.
A stop written
.Ql \(pl Ns Ar n
is
.Ar n
columns after the one before it, and
.Ql T Ar n
repeats every
.Ar n
columns after the last one.
Past the last stop a tab moves nowhere.
.El
.Pp
Other typesetting requests, such as
.Ic \&ad ,
.Ic \&na ,
.Ic \&hy ,
.Ic \&nh ,
.Ic \&ne
and
.Ic \&ll ,
are accepted and have no effect.
A page cannot redefine the
.Nm
macros with
.Ic \&de ;
such definitions are ignored.
.Pp
The number register
.Va an-margin
holds the left margin set by
.Ic \&RS
and
.Ic \&RE ,
in basic units (24 to a column), as groff's man macros keep it.
.Sh SCALING UNITS
Widths and distances are numbers with an optional unit:
.Bl -column "Unit" "one column (default for widths)" -compact
.It Sy Unit Ta Sy Meaning
.It Li n , m Ta one column (default for widths)
.It Li v Ta one line (default for vertical distances)
.It Li i Ta an inch, ten columns
.It Li c Ta a centimetre
.It Li p , P Ta a point, a pica
.It Li u Ta a basic unit, 1/24 of a column
.El
Only a leading number and unit are read; anything after them, such as the
rest of
.Ql 3n+2n ,
is ignored.
Fractions are rounded to whole columns or lines, an exact half down.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr man 1 ,
.Xr oxdoc 1 ,
.Xr mdoc 7 ,
.Xr roff 7 ,
.Xr tbl 7
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
language first appeared in
.At v7 ,
replacing the
.Nm
macros of earlier editions.
The
.Ic \&SY ,
.Ic \&OP ,
.Ic \&EX ,
.Ic \&UR ,
.Ic \&MT
and
.Ic \&MR
macros are extensions from GNU troff.
This manual was written for OxideBSD.
